@reporter 
you may set the datatype and column name by
`select column0::VARCHAR reference_pool_id, column1::VARCHAR loan_identifier from 'a.csv'`

Thanks for the fix, @participant3.

I now run the following code with index in double quotes.

```sql
SET threads = 1;
PRAGMA memory_limit = '24GB';
PRAGMA temp_directory = 'data/temp/';
COPY (
  SELECT column000::VARCHAR reference_pool_id, column001::VARCHAR loan_identifier, column002::DATE monthly_reporting_period, ... column093::VARCHAR "index", ...
  FROM read_csv_auto('data/stage/*.csv',
	  HEADER = FALSE, DATEFORMAT = '%m%Y', DELIM = '|', SAMPLE_SIZE = 500000)
) TO 'data/public/performance.parquet' (FORMAT PARQUET);  
```

If I just try to read the first four files (e.g., `read_csv_auto('data/stage/2000*.csv' ...`), the code runs fine.  Nice!

However, when I try to expand the glob to read all of the files (e.g., `read_csv_auto('data/stage/*.csv' ...`), I get a version of the "Conversion error" above.

`"Conversion Error: date field value out of range: "072021", expected format is (YYYY-MM-DD)"`

I don't understand why the DATEFORMAT option somehow stops working when applied to all 93 files via globbing.

try to add a year column
```
create table orders(m int,v int);
insert into orders select i%12+1,i from range(100)t(i);
copy (select 2000+(v//12)y,m,v from orders) TO 'orders1' (FORMAT PARQUET, PARTITION_BY (y));
```

try to use 10 no partitioned parquet files to ouput the partitioned files. Use filename as the extra partition key. 
such as
copy (select 'file1' filename,*from 'file1.parquet' ) to TO 'orders1' (FORMAT PARQUET, PARTITION_BY (filename,y));
copy (select 'file2' filename,*from 'file2.parquet' ) to TO 'orders1' (FORMAT PARQUET, PARTITION_BY (filename,y));
